 !!! Note
     The minimum last stake amount can be configured using `last_stake_amount_min_ratio` - which defaults to 0.5 (50%). This means that the minimum stake amount that's ever used is `stake_amount * 0.5`. This avoids very low stake amounts, that are close to the minimum tradable amount for the pair and can be refused by the exchange.
 
+#### Max underlying exposure
+
+If you trade several pairs that are all priced against, or backed by, the same underlying currency (for example a handful of futures all margined in BTC, or several coins that are all quoted in the same stablecoin), a big move in that one currency can hit several of your open trades at once - even though each individual trade looked fine in isolation.
+
+`max_underlying_exposure` lets you cap this concentration risk: it's the maximum share of your tradable capital that may be committed, at the same time, to trades whose pair shares the same underlying (quote/settlement) currency.
+
+For example, with `max_underlying_exposure=0.5` and a tradable capital of 1000 USDT, no more than 500 USDT may be committed at once to USDT-quoted trades combined - regardless of how many individual trades that spans, and regardless of `max_open_trades` or `stake_amount`.
+
+When opening a new trade (or increasing an existing position) would push the combined exposure to that currency above the configured share, the stake amount is reduced to fit under the limit, the same way it would be for e.g. `last_stake_amount_min_ratio`. If no room is left at all, the trade is skipped. The reason is logged the same way as other entry-sizing decisions.
+
+This setting is disabled by default (no cap applied) and is evaluated identically in live/dry-run and in backtesting.
+
 #### Static stake amount
 
 The `stake_amount` configuration statically configures the amount of stake-currency your bot will use for each trade.
